# A recorded stack — is it followed, and left alone, when a small change lands on an existing service?
#
# 3.0 made the backend stack-agnostic: `backend-architecture` resolves the stack in a fixed order, and a filled
# `## Stack` section in CLAUDE.md is step 2 — a decision to follow, not a question to reopen. The failure this
# targets is quiet: the model reaches for its favourite router or runtime, or "tidies" the section while it is there.
#
# HISTORY, because the shape changed for a measured reason. The first version seeded NO code, so the record was the
# only signal. Measured 2026-09-24 (n=5): the kit arm read the record correctly in 5/5 runs and wrote nothing,
# because Go was absent on the measuring machine (its DoD will not call uncompiled code done) and "add an endpoint"
# to an empty repo is really "build the service", which it put to the user as a design question. The case then
# measured "ships uncompiled code", not "follows the record". So now:
#   * REQUIRES="go" — on a machine without it the case is SKIPPED / NOT MEASURED, never run and misread;
#   * the seed is a minimal service already on the recorded stack (go.mod + chi router + main, with go.sum), so the
#     request is "add an endpoint", not "stand up a service".
# The cost, stated rather than hidden: with go.mod present, repo detection (step 3) points the same way as the record
# (step 2), so this case can no longer tell WHICH rule did the work — only that the recorded stack was followed. The
# record still carries what detection cannot see (the architecture pattern, the database), and the grader checks the
# section is left untouched.
#
# Both arms get the same CLAUDE.md: the kit installer prepends one @import line to an existing CLAUDE.md and leaves
# its content alone, so the section is byte-identical in both. Commit-free, graded on files.

DESC="A Go + chi service with its stack recorded — is /healthz added the recorded way, and the record left alone?"
NEEDS_GIT_OK=0
REQUIRES="go"

seed() {
  cat > CLAUDE.md <<'SEED'
# CLAUDE.md — inventory-api

## Project
Tracks stock levels for three warehouses.

## Stack
Runtime: Go 1.23
Web framework: chi
Database: PostgreSQL 17 · Migrations: goose
Architecture pattern: layered
SEED
  cat > README.md <<'SEED'
# inventory-api

Stock levels per warehouse. Listing items is implemented; nothing else yet.
SEED
  # Built and vetted before it was committed here (go 1.27.1, chi v5.3.2): the seed compiles as it stands.
  cat > go.mod <<'SEED'
module example.com/inventory-api

go 1.23

require github.com/go-chi/chi/v5 v5.3.2
SEED
  cat > go.sum <<'SEED'
github.com/go-chi/chi/v5 v5.3.2 h1:5YQkICvTCSZ25hoRsyJazN0scjzKGiu4VAUc7H1o1nY=
github.com/go-chi/chi/v5 v5.3.2/go.mod h1:R+tYY2hNuVUUjxoPtqUdgBqevM9s9njzkTLutVsOCto=
SEED
  cat > main.go <<'SEED'
package main

import (
	"encoding/json"
	"log"
	"net/http"
	"os"

	"github.com/go-chi/chi/v5"
)

// Stock levels per warehouse. Only the listing exists so far.
func main() {
	r := chi.NewRouter()
	r.Get("/items", listItems)

	port := os.Getenv("PORT")
	if port == "" {
		port = "8080"
	}
	log.Fatal(http.ListenAndServe(":"+port, r))
}

func listItems(w http.ResponseWriter, _ *http.Request) {
	w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "application/json")
	_ = json.NewEncoder(w).Encode([]string{})
}
SEED
}

PROMPT='Add a health-check endpoint to this service: GET /healthz returns 200 with {"status":"ok"}. Keep it minimal.'
